    @section sqlite3intro sqlite3 Module Introduction

    The \c sqlite3 module provides an <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/index.html">SQLite v3</a> driver to %Qore, allowing
    %Qore programs to access SQLite3 database files and/or in-memory databases through the \c %Datasource and
    \c %DatasourcePool classes.

    This module is released under a choice of two licenses:
    - <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a>
    - MIT (see COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for more information)
    .
    The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how
    the %Qore library was initialized).

    Example of creating a SQLite Datasource:
    @code{.py}
# user and password are not present as they are ignored
Datasource ds("sqlite3:@/tmp/my-file.sqlite(utf8)");
    @endcode

    The DB name can be a real path to an SQLite3 DB file or the \c ":memory:" string to use an empty in-memory
    database. Please refer to the SQLite3 documentation for more information on how the SQLite library opens
    databases.

    The driver supports transactions, bound parameters, and prepared statements.
    See @ref sqlite3capabilitiesguide for supported features and their requirements.

    The driver employs efficient binary bindings for all supported data types when selecting and binding by value.

    The \c Datasource::getServerVersion() method is implemented for this driver and returns a string in
    \c MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH notation - e.g. \c "3.6.7".  \c Datasource::getClientVersion() returns the same information.

    Like all Qore components, the \c sqlite3 driver is thread-safe.

    @subsection sqlite3sandbox Sandboxing

    The database file of a connection is checked against the filesystem policy of a sandbox (for reading, writing and
    creating it); only the exact name \c ":memory:" opens an in-memory database without a check.  A database name
    that is a URI (\c "file:...") is refused under a sandbox that restricts network or filesystem access, as it can
    name a file, options and storage (another VFS, a shared in-memory database) that the policy cannot check.  In a
    %Qore version that checks DBI connections against the sandbox, the policy is that of the code that chose the
    file (that created the datasource or set its database name), also when other code opens the connection: a file
    that sandboxed code set on a trusted datasource is refused, and a trusted datasource (or pool) that sandboxed
    code opens is not; in an earlier version, the policy of the code that opens the connection.

    The files that SQL opens are checked against the sandbox of the code that runs the statement:
    - \c ATTACH checks its file like the database when the statement is compiled; a file name that is not a literal
      (an expression or a bound value), or that is a URI, cannot be checked and is refused.  \c ":memory:" and the
      empty name (a temporary database) are allowed
    - \c VACUUM \c INTO checks its target (also one given as an expression or a bound value) when it runs, and
      refuses a URI
    - setting \c PRAGMA \c temp_store_directory or \c data_store_directory is refused

    A refused statement raises a \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED exception.  SQLite also creates files next to the
    database (its journal, write-ahead log and lock files) and temporary files in the system's temporary directory,
    which are not checked separately.

    On Linux, with a %Qore version that pins the local path of a connection (\c QORE_HAS_DBI_PIN_CONNECT_PATH), the
    database of a connection that a sandbox restricts is opened in the directory that was checked: the database file
    and the files that SQLite creates next to it (journal, write-ahead log and shared memory) are opened in that
    directory, by name, without following a symbolic link, whatever its path names after the check; the name of
    the database that SQLite reports (\c PRAGMA \c database_list) is then a \c /proc/self/fd/ path.  Otherwise, and
    for \c ATTACH, a file is checked by its path, so a file that is replaced after the check (a symbolic link to a
    file outside the sandbox root, made by code that can create one inside it) is opened as it is then; an
    \c ATTACH file is checked when the statement is compiled and opened when it runs, which for a prepared statement
    (SQLStatement) can be much later.

